Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Sudden anosmia/ageusia, Dry continuous cough, High fever/myalgia, Bilateral peripheral ground-glass opacities

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-003
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and high fever in the context of viral illness, requiring urgent auscultation and oxygen saturation assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated progressive dyspnoea, continuous coughing, and high fever. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for hypoxaemia and impending respiratory failure that could not be safely delayed until the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't catch my breath, my chest burns when I cough, and I can't taste anything."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 4 days ago with an acute onset of high fever, severe recurrent chills, and profound systemic myalgia. Over the past 48 hours, the patient developed a dry, continuous, and hacking non-productive cough, alongside a sudden and profound loss of taste (ageusia) and smell (anosmia). Today, the patient notes progressive dyspnoea upon minimal exertion. Patient also reports severe generalized fatigue.
Functional Status: Confined to an armchair, breathless upon walking to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of household transmission; caregiver is an older adult child.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 93% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Fatigued, using accessory muscles mildly during speech, flushed.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals tachypnoea and scattered fine bilateral basal crackles. No wheeze. Cardiovascular exam reveals regular tachycardia. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Sudden anosmia/ageusia, Dry continuous cough, High fever/myalgia (radiology for bilateral peripheral ground-glass opacities deferred to hospital if required).
Environmental Observations: Poor ventilation in the room; advised opening windows.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: COVID-19 - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of productive, purulent sputum and the presence of prominent anosmia/ageusia.

Influenza A/B - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Specific loss of taste and smell and precipitous drop in oxygen saturation upon exertion, which is classic for COVID-19.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of infective symptoms, high fever, and absence of unilateral leg swelling or sudden pleuritic chest pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with progressive dyspnoea, tachypnoea, and borderline hypoxaemia (SpO2 93%) secondary to a presumed acute viral pneumonitis. Given the known risk of precipitous respiratory deterioration (silent hypoxia) in this clinical context, an urgent physical respiratory assessment and baseline oximetry were mandatory. The inability to safely monitor this patient's respiratory reserve remotely necessitated an urgent after-hours visit to prevent critical hypoxic injury prior to the next in-hours period (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administer PCR swab. Instruct on proning techniques to improve V/Q matching. Prescribe inhaled budesonide to reduce pulmonary inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By practicing deep diaphragmatic breathing and incentive spirometry every hour, the patient will actively expand the lung bases, which will reward them with visibly improved oxygen saturations on their home monitor and reduced breathlessness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Delivery of a home pulse oximeter.
DME/Safety (Optional): Home pulse oximeter.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 24 hours to monitor SpO2 trajectory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if resting oxygen saturation falls below 92%, if there is new-onset confusion, central cyanosis (blue lips/face), or an inability to complete short sentences without pausing for breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending RT-PCR confirmation; respiratory status is dynamic.
Clinical Course: Days 5 to 10 are the highest risk period for respiratory decompensation. Careful monitoring is required.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call for an ambulance immediately if you experience worsening chest pain, severe shortness of breath at rest, or if your oxygen monitor consistently reads below 92%.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The inhaled steroid will locally reduce lung inflammation without systemic side effects. Prone positioning helps distribute oxygen to the healthier parts of the back of your lungs.
Actionable Steps: Sleep on your stomach or side, not flat on your back. Monitor your oxygen levels three times daily and record the numbers.

